CSS BACKGROUND-IMAGE 
====================

BACKGROUND-IMAGE
================
background-image places an image
behind the content of an element.

Key points
----------
- Does not affect layout
- Does not take space
- Appears behind text
- Used only for decoration


BASIC SYNTAX
============
.element {
    background-image: url("image.jpg");
}


IMAGE SOURCES
=============
You can use
- Local images
- Online images
- CSS gradients


LOCAL IMAGE
-----------
.box {
    background-image: url("images/bg.png");
}

Tip:
If the image is not visible,
check the image path and height.


ONLINE IMAGE
------------
.card {
    background-image: url("https://example.com/image.jpg");
}

Note:
Requires internet connection.


GRADIENT AS IMAGE
-----------------
.banner {
    background-image: linear-gradient(red, orange);
}

Note:
Gradients are also background images.


MULTIPLE BACKGROUND IMAGES
=========================
The first image is on top.

.layered {
    background-image:
        url("top.png"),
        url("bottom.jpg");
}


REMOVE BACKGROUND IMAGE
======================
.clear-bg {
    background-image: none;
}


BACKGROUND-IMAGE vs <img>
========================
Use background-image
- Decorative images
- No SEO
- No layout impact

Use <img>
- Content images
- SEO friendly
- Part of layout


COMMON MISTAKES
===============
- Wrong image path
- Missing height
- Expecting layout changes
- Using background-image for content


PRACTICE
========
Create a hero section
with height 300px
and a background image.

.hero {
    height: 300px;
    background-image: url("hero.jpg");
}
